Measles
Paediatric & Neonatal
Koplik spots, Cephalocaudal maculopapular rash, Cough/Coryza/Conjunctivitis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-029
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of a spreading rash, high fever, and severe lethargy in an unimmunized child, requiring urgent assessment to rule out meningococcemia or severe atypical pneumonia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a highly toxic-appearing child with a spreading rash, severe cough, and red eyes. Immediate assessment was required to strictly exclude rapidly fatal meningococcal septicemia, evaluate the degree of respiratory compromise, and implement immediate airborne infection control protocols prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The rash started on his face this morning and is spreading downwards, and he has a terrible cough and red, watery eyes."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a classic prodromal triad of a harsh cough, severe coryza (runny nose), and non-purulent conjunctivitis (the 3 Cs) that began four days ago. Over the last 24 hours, there has been an abrupt onset of a high-grade fever peaking simultaneously with the eruption of an erythematous, confluent maculopapular rash. The caregivers explicitly note the rash began precisely on the face and hairline, spreading cephalocaudally down the trunk. The patient has severe photophobia and tearing. The patient exhibits marked systemic toxicity and lethargy highly uncharacteristic of a simple viral illness. The parents confirm the child's vaccination status is strictly unimmunized due to philosophical objection.
Functional Status: Lethargic, photophobic, bed-bound.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high risk of airborne contagious transmission to the community; risk of severe viral pneumonia or encephalitis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 135 | Temp: 39.8C | RR: 28 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 16 Kg
General: Toxic appearing, lethargic, actively coughing, averting eyes from room light.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal examination explicitly reveals Koplik spots manifesting as tiny bluish-white grains of sand on an erythematous base strictly located on the buccal mucosa opposite the molars. Cutaneous examination shows a vivid, confluent maculopapular rash on the face, neck, and upper trunk, blanching upon pressure. Ocular examination shows severe bilateral conjunctivitis with copious tearing. Respiratory exam reveals coarse upper airway transmitted sounds with scattered bilateral crackles, indicating early lower respiratory tract involvement. Generalized prominent cervical lymphadenopathy is present. The diagnostic tetrad is unequivocally confirmed.
Environmental Observations: Sibling (also unimmunized) is playing in the same room.
Medication Reconciliation: Refusing oral fluids and paracetamol.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Measles (Rubeola) - Status: Active infectious phase with suspected early pneumonitis
Differentials:

Meningococcal Disease - Ruled out explicitly by the rash blanching upon pressure, presence of a severe cough/coryza prodrome, and the presence of Koplik spots.

Rubella (German Measles) - Ruled out by the extreme severity of the systemic toxicity, high fever, and the presence of Koplik spots (rubella is typically much milder and features prominent suboccipital nodes).

Roseola Infantum - Ruled out by the concurrent presentation of the high fever and the rash; in roseola, the rash characteristically appears strictly after the fever abruptly breaks.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Critical public health threat, severe medical risk to patient)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of a highly toxic, unimmunized child with a descending maculopapular rash and high fever mandated an urgent, highly controlled after-hours assessment. Telephone triage accurately prioritized the need to rule out meningococcal sepsis. Objective clinical evaluation definitively diagnosed measles based on the pathognomonic Koplik spots and classic clinical progression. Delaying this assessment would have subjected the patient to unrecognized respiratory compromise and allowed for the unchecked, catastrophic airborne spread of the virus within the community. Immediate clinical intervention secured the diagnosis, legally mandated public health notification, and initiated absolute respiratory isolation protocols.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated strict airborne isolation protocols immediately. Administered stat dose of Vitamin A (200,000 IU) to reduce morbidity. Legally mandated notification generated to the Department of Health.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the severe photophobia, immediately turn off all overhead lighting and close all blinds in the child's room. Rewarding the child's complaints of eye pain with immediate environmental darkness physically eliminates the noxious stimulus, dramatically reducing the child's agitation and allowing for restorative sleep while isolated.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent public health contact tracing.
DME/Safety (Optional): N95 masks provided to caregivers.
Follow-up: Mandatory daily GP or Public Health telehealth review to monitor for pneumonia or encephalitis.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops severe difficulty breathing, a severe headache, sudden vomiting, extreme drowsiness, or generalized seizures.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Measles is a profoundly contagious, legally notifiable disease with high rates of severe complications.
Clinical Course: The fever and rash will peak over the next 48 hours. The cough may persist for weeks. Pneumonia and brain swelling are life-threatening complications that can develop suddenly.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if the child struggles to breathe, becomes extremely confused, or has a seizure. Notify the ambulance dispatcher that the child has confirmed Measles.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The child has a severe viral infection that suppresses the immune system. There is no antibiotic cure. The high-dose Vitamin A helps the body fight the severe complications. The virus spreads through the air and stays in an empty room for up to two hours.
Actionable Steps: Keep the child strictly isolated in a dark room. No visitors are permitted under any circumstances. Ensure the unimmunized sibling is quarantined immediately.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: Confirmed Measles. Notifiable disease. Strict isolation required. Vitamin A given. Monitor closely for breathing issues.

SMS EDUCATION

